WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.290
The previous video got us introduced to Meiosis!It
is defined as an exceptional

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:15.210
phenomenon which produces FOUR daughter cells,
each with half the number of

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.990
chromosomes, compared to the single parent
cell!

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.950
Meiosis is a process that gives rise to a
haploid cell from diploid one.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.330
We have
seen these definitions!

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.950
Now before moving ahead with the actual process,
let us

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.790
halt at the question that we left you with
in the previous video!

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:40.230
We need to know, which are the reproductive
cells that we have been talking

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.270
about so far?

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.780
Which are the cells with half the number of
chromosomes?

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.840
Let us understand that first!

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.790
The Egg cell of female and Sperm cell of male,
are haploid cells which unite to

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.090
form a Zygote.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.530
And these haploid cells together are called
“Sex cells” or “Gametes

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.460
or even Germ cells”.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.610
Now let us understand how these gametes are
produced with the help of

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.710
Meiosis!!

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:14.080
Just like mitosis, the cell during meiosis,
also goes through a number of

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.490
orchestrated steps.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.560
So does that mean Meiosis is just like Mitosis?

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.420
Kind of...!

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:26.710
Meiosis is a lot like mitosis, but they have
some major differences as well.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.760
Let us list down the similarities between
Mitosis and Meiosis first!

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.770
The cell goes through similar stages and uses
the same strategies to organize and

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.260
separate chromosomes.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.030
It goes through the Interphase for its growth
and DNA

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.030
replication.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:50.150
Later, it enters the dividing stage that is
Karyokinesis followed by

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.170
Cytokinesis!

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.250
Both the processes require ample amount of
time to prepare for the

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.710
actual division.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.009
These were the similarities between the two
processes.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:04.900
Now what could be the differences between
them?

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.310
Let us have a glance at them.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.290
In meiosis, the cell has a more complex task
to perform.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.209
Don’t worry ...This
doesn’t mean that these tasks are difficult

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:16.209
to study.

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:21.420
The tasks are difficult for the
cells, not for us!

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.819
They are way too simple if you understand
the concept well.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:28.510
Let us get to know
these tasks!

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:32.499
We know that mitosis involves four stages
for cell division!

00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:36.840
Can you try and
recollect the four stages of mitosis?

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.469
That’s a right answer!

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:43.459
They are Prophase,
Metaphase, Anaphase and Telophase.

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:48.809
On similar lines, meiosis also has four
stages for cell division.

00:02:48.809 --> 00:02:53.469
But here, the slight difference is that meiosis
is carried out

00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:55.809
in two rounds or steps.

00:02:55.809 --> 00:02:59.541
That is, Meiosis is divided into “Meiosis-one”
and

00:02:59.541 --> 00:03:03.419
“Meiosis-two” as two major phases.

00:03:03.419 --> 00:03:07.129
But why do cells need two rounds of division
here?

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:11.480
The simple answer is, we need four daughter
cells with half the number of

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.650
chromosomes each!

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.209
So the first step gives us two cells, which
divide further to

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:18.849
give the four cells.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:24.559
Technically speaking, the first round separates
the homologous chromosomes,

00:03:24.559 --> 00:03:29.519
while the second round separates the sister
chromatids from each other.

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:33.920
We are familiar with the terms “homologous
chromosomes”, and sister

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.339
chromatids...right?

00:03:35.339 --> 00:03:37.220
But let us revise them, once!

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:42.279
The term “homologous” means the ones having
the same relation, position or

00:03:42.279 --> 00:03:43.279
structure.

00:03:43.279 --> 00:03:48.209
So here, it represents the two chromosomes
forming a pair.

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:53.419
In other words, when we say a “pair of chromosomes”,
we actually address a pair

00:03:53.419 --> 00:03:57.459
that has one chromosome from mom, and one
from dad.

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:00.389
But can any two
chromosomes form a pair?

00:04:00.389 --> 00:04:01.389
No!

00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:08.430
The chromosomes forming a pair should be same
in size, structure, length and even the loci

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.010
of their genes.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:14.140
Thus, only similar ones
can form a pair!

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:18.560
So homologous chromosomes mean the set of
twin chromosomes, obtained one

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.799
from mom and one from dad!

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:25.150
The next term is “sister chromatids”.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:29.980
In this single chromosome structure, these
two thread-like arms attached to this central

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:34.980
bulge called “centromere” are called
the sister chromatids.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.030
This sister chromatid is like an exact copy
of this one, as it

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.160
contains the replicated DNA.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.810
Now getting back to the division process!

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:51.220
In order to carry out the separation of
homologous chromosomes and then the sister

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.460
chromatids, the cell has to
undergo two rounds of division.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:59.560
So the cell first enters meiosis-one!

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.600
Here it begins
with an amazing new journey of division involving

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.200
four stages namely Prophase-
one, Metaphase-one, Anaphase-one and Telophase-one!

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.530
Let us learn about them
in detail in the next video!

